{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "The lighthouse keeper climbed the spiral staircase every evening at dusk, counting the one hundred and twelve iron steps as his father had taught him. At the top, he polished the great glass lens until it gleamed, trimmed the wick, and checked the brass clockwork that turned the beam in slow, steady circles across the water. The station had stood on the rocky point for more than a century, guiding fishing boats and cargo ships past the reef that had claimed so many vessels in the old days. A logbook sat on the desk beside the window, its pages filled with careful handwriting: wind speed, visibility, passing ships, and the occasional note about a storm that rattled the tower until dawn. The coast guard had sent letters explaining that automated lights were cheaper and more reliable, and that his position would soon be unnecessary. He read each letter twice, folded it neatly, and tucked it into a drawer with the others. In the mornings he walked the shoreline, gathering driftwood and greeting the fishermen who waved from their boats. They brought him mackerel and news from the village, and he offered them coffee brewed on his small iron stove. Children sometimes hiked out to the point on summer afternoons, and he let them peer through the lens at the endless gray horizon. He told them stories of shipwrecks and rescues, of the night a trawler limped home through the fog, following nothing but his light. When darkness fell again, he climbed the stairs, patient and unhurried, and set the beam turning once more."}
